WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.400
Sony and Disney found their way back to the
bargaining table, and a deal has been struck.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.420
Ol' web-head will get at least one more MCU-set
starring vehicle, one that we have a funny

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.360
feeling will have the word "home" somewhere
in its title.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.780
These are just a few things you can expect.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:20.960
Details about Spider-Man's third solo outing
in the MCU are exceedingly slim.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.740
Surprisingly, though, we already know the
film's release date, as it was announced at

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:28.580
the same time that Marvel and Sony went public
with the renewing of their shared custody

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:29.400
deal.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.660
Spider-Man's next web-slinging adventure will
hit the big screen on July 16, 2021, at least,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.100
that's where it's slotted at the moment.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.940
It's possible it could be pushed off to a
later date, for a couple of reasons.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.520
First, Marvel's 2021 slate is already pretty
crowded.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.600
Previously announced to be on tap for that
year are Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.780
Rings on February 12, Doctor Strange in the
Multiverse of Madness on May 7, and Thor:

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.500
Love and Thunder on November 5.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.320
If Spidey 3's release date holds up, it would
mark the first time Marvel has released four

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:04.700
MCU pictures in the same year, which even
the most hardcore fans might see as crowding

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:05.560
the market.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.960
And that's before we even get to that year's
Disney+ output.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.480
Also, that summer will see the release of
another highly anticipated superhero film:

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.890
Matt Reeves' The Batman, which hits screens
on June 25.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:21.720
The Robert Pattinson-starring flick will be
the first Batman solo film in nearly a decade.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.020
While Marvel has every reason to be confident
in the web-slinger's ability to kill it at

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:29.820
the box office, there would be no shame in
rethinking putting him up against the Dark

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:30.480
Knight.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:31.900
"No, no, no, no.

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:33.860
That's…no, that's definitely not what you
wanna watch."

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:38.880
Plot details for the third MCU Spider-Man
flick have not been divulged, in all likelihood

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.740
because the movie is still tangled up in early
development.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.400
We do know one thing, however: life is about
to get even more difficult for Peter Parker.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.940
Spider-Man: Far From Home ended on a heck
of a cliffhanger, with Parker's identity having

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.840
been revealed to the public thanks to Mysterio
and J. Jonah Jameson.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:00.460
In the MCU, Jameson is apparently a conspiracy
theorist vlogger and not a newspaperman, but

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.140
he still looks a heck of a lot like J.K. Simmons.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.360
"Here's the real blockbuster.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:05.370
Brace yourselves.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:06.400
You might want to sit down.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.020
Spider-Man's name is Peter Parker!"

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.200
Of course, we can speculate.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.960
Outside of the third Tom Holland movie, Sony
is continuing to develop its Spidey-related

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.700
properties independently of involvement from
Marvel Studios.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.950
At the moment, those projects include Venom
2, Morbius, and Kraven the Hunter,

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:30.520
which recently found a screenwriter in The
Equalizer series' scribe Richard Wenk.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:34.960
It should be noted that, since the days of
the aborted Andrew Garfield-starring Amazing

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:39.800
Spider-Man franchise, Sony has been keen to
get the Sinister Six, a revolving team of

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.640
ol' webhead's greatest nemeses, onto the screen.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.020
"First and final warning.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.020
Stay out of our way."

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.760
With all of the "Spider-wheelings and dealings"
that have gone on in recent months, fans could

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.260
be forgiven for being a bit confused as to
the nature of Sony and Marvel's relationship

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.140
at this point.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:06.840
Follow us here: Venom, which starred Tom Hardy
and featured exactly no references to Spider-Man,

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:13.110
surprised everyone by grossing over $850 million
worldwide despite lukewarm reviews.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.460
Morbius, a solo vehicle for the "Living Vampire"
and longtime Spidey nemesis which Sony has

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.400
on tap for 2020, may very well follow suit.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.860
Both films are part of the thus far Spidey-less
Sony Universe of Marvel Characters.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.640
Everyone knows that the MCU reigns supreme,
so why not engineer a crossover between the

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:31.720
two universes?

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.620
In fact, this may very well be what's happening,
and if so, Spider-Man 3 will likely to be

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:38.460
a focal point of the strategy.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:43.560
No less an authority than Marvel Studios head
honcho Kevin Feige has teased that Spidey

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:48.381
is, quote, "the only hero with the superpower
to cross cinematic universes," and in the

00:03:48.381 --> 00:03:52.560
past, he's suggested that Hardy's Venom and
Holland's Spidey could one day meet on the

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.480
big screen.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.820
It's all but certain that the two studios
are busy hashing out the details even as you're

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:02.060
watching this, but for now, we'll just have
to wait to see how it all plays out.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.500
One of Spider-Man: Far From Home's biggest
twists came during the flick's post-credits

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.480
scene, when it was revealed that the Nick
Fury and Maria Hill we'd seen for the entirety

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.940
of the film were, in fact, Skrull impostors
assigned to Parker by the real Fury, who was

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:21.600
busy...well, in space, tending to some secret
mission or another on a ship full of Skrulls.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.000
All of this while being extremely shoe-less.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.080
"Who's got my shoes?"

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.640
Jokes aside, this set off a massive wave of
speculation that the flick had been tasked

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:35.740
with doing a bit of heavy lifting in setting
up an MCU adaptation of the Secret Invasion

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.210
storyline, in which Skrulls infiltrate the
highest ranks of power on Earth by way of,

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.820
you know, imitating everyone.

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.000
Remarks made by director Jon Watts seem to
indicate, though, that the twist was actually

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.780
his decision, and not a mandate from on high.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.880
This would likely mean that Fury's space jaunt
isn't, in fact, setting up major future developments

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.810
for the MCU as a whole, so, could it have
been teasing a continued role for Fury and/or

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:00.940
the Skrulls in the next Spider-Man movie?

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:05.410
For now, it's completely unknown, but our
money is on the superspy returning to Earth

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:09.420
to resume his stewardship of Parker, with
a few interesting stories to tell.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.920
When Sony and Marvel made up, the first question
on fans' minds was the obvious one: for how

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:14.740
long?

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.860
All we know for certain is that, aside from
his third solo venture, Spidey will appear

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.040
in at least one more Marvel Studios film.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.380
Beyond that, the future is a question mark.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.500
Considering that the MCU and Sony's Universe
of Marvel Characters are finally making overtures

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:32.500
toward recognizing each other's existence,
though, there are a few ways it's likely to

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.020
go.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:37.280
The studios could, as previously mentioned,
find a viable narrative way for the character

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.080
to switch cinematic universes.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:43.930
Or, as Deadline has suggest, they could form
a loose connection, with a kind of "call and

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:48.780
response" aesthetic which would keep the franchises
largely separate, but tangentially connected.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:53.200
Or, if the two studios can truly see their
way clear to giving the fans what they want,

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:57.940
the two universes could merge, with Sony's
established characters being absorbed into

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:58.880
the MCU.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.280
It seems like a pretty slight possibility,
and it might be, but it also happens to be

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.100
the option likely to generate the maximum
amount of box office dollars, so we're going

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.380
to go ahead and call it "not impossible."

